{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a thought-provoking film that delves into the world of social media and its impact on human relationships. directed by david fincher and written by acid york, the movie tells the story of mark zuckerberg, the co-founder and former ceo of facebook.\n\nthe film begins with mark (played by jesse eisenberg) as a young college student who creates a website called \"facemash\" to compare the photos of two students and allow users to vote on which one was more attractive. the site becomes popular, but also attracts unwanted attention from the college administration. mark is subsequently banned from the college and forced to drop out, but he soon decides to create a new website called \"thefacebook.\"\n\nwith the help of his roommates and fellow harvard students eduardo savarin (andrew garfield) and divya (daisy dukes), mark navigates the complexities of creating a successful social network. he faces opposition from his roommates, who want to take control of the site, and from the university, which is concerned about the site's impact on campus life.\n\nas the story unfolds, the film takes a darker turn, revealing the cutthroat world of startup culture and the often-cutthroat nature of the tech industry. mark's relationships with his friends and loved ones begin to suffer, and he finds himself isolated and alone.\n\nthe film is strong on performances, with jesse eisenberg delivering a standout performance as mark zuckerberg. andrew garfield is also excellent as eduardo, bringing a sense of vulnerability and sensitivity to his portrayal. the chemistry between the leads is palpable, and their performances add depth and nuance to the film.\n\nthe film's direction is also noteworthy, with fincher using a combination of close-ups and wide shots to create a sense of intimacy and isolation. the score, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, 